-- ukraine-nuclear-accident-risk-rises-winter-20261119 (news; English 726 words; Nepali 473 words)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ukraine-nuclear-accident-risk-rises-winter-20261119', 'news', 'Ukraine',
'Ukraine Nuclear Accident Risk Rises This Winter',
'यस जाडोमा युक्रेनमा आणविक दुर्घटनाको जोखिम बढ्यो',
'Ukraine nuclear accident risk is rising this winter, regulators from 38 countries warned, urging Russia to stop strikes on the power grid keeping reactors cool.',
'३८ देशका नियामकहरूले चेतावनी दिएअनुसार यस जाडोमा युक्रेनमा आणविक दुर्घटनाको जोखिम बढिरहेको छ; रिएक्टर चिसो राख्ने विद्युत् ग्रिडमा आक्रमण रोक्न रुसलाई आग्रह गरिएको छ।',
$$<p>The Ukraine nuclear accident risk is rising this winter, nuclear safety regulators representing 38 countries warned on Monday 16 November, urging Russia to stop its attacks on the power infrastructure that keeps Ukrainian reactors cool.</p><p>The warning came in a joint statement from three bodies, HERCA, WENRA and NORA, seen by <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/risk-nuclear-accident-ukraine-increasing-this-winter-atomic-safety-regulators-2026-11-16/">Reuters</a> before publication. Together they represent all European Union countries plus the United States, Britain, Canada and Japan, among others.</p><h2>Ukraine nuclear accident risk at a glance</h2><table><tr><th>Detail</th><th>Information</th></tr><tr><td>Warning</td><td>Risk of a nuclear accident in Ukraine increasing this winter, 16 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Issued by</td><td>Regulators from 38 countries, through HERCA, WENRA and NORA</td></tr><tr><td>Core concern</td><td>Unstable grid power makes it harder to keep nuclear fuel cool and avoid a meltdown</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear plants</td><td>Four plants, three operating, providing about 80 percent of winter power</td></tr><tr><td>Worst case</td><td>Nationwide blackout hitting all nine producing reactors at once</td></tr><tr><td>Zaporizhzhia</td><td>Europe largest plant, under Russian control, has lost off-site power 27 times</td></tr></table><h2>What happened</h2><p>Moscow has stepped up drone and missile attacks on Ukrainian energy installations, such as plants, power lines and electricity substations, a system already weakened after nearly five years of conflict. The regulators said they expressed deep concern about the increasing risks to nuclear safety arising from Russia war against Ukraine, and its continued attacks on the energy infrastructure, and saw an urgent need for Russia to cease attacks against nuclear facilities and the energy infrastructure essential to their safe operation.</p><p>Their key concern is cooling. Without a stable electricity supply from the grid, it becomes more and more difficult for Ukrainian nuclear plants to keep their nuclear material cool and avoid a meltdown. If that were to occur, there could be releases of radioactivity into the air as happened during the 1986 Chornobyl disaster, considered the world worst civil nuclear accident, which spread iodine-131, caesium-134 and caesium-137 across parts of Ukraine, Belarus, Russia, and northern and central Europe.</p><p>Per Strand, head of HERCA and NORA, told <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/risk-nuclear-accident-ukraine-increasing-this-winter-atomic-safety-regulators-2026-11-16/">Reuters</a> that Russian attacks had become deadlier in recent weeks, with drones that carry bigger payloads and are more precise in their targeting. He said the highest risk will be this winter, and described the worst case as a nationwide blackout affecting all nine producing reactors at the same time. He stressed the situation could be avoided if action was taken now and Russia was pressured to stop its attacks on energy infrastructure.</p><p>On Saturday 14 November, the International Atomic Energy Agency said military activity had caused instability in Ukraine electricity in the preceding week, resulting in power reductions at some operating units. Ukraine has four nuclear power plants, of which three are in operation, providing some 80 percent of the power used this winter. Its fourth, Zaporizhzhia, Europe largest, is under Russian control near the front line. It is not currently producing electricity but relies on external power to keep nuclear material cool. Since the start of the war it has lost off-site power 27 times, forcing it to switch to emergency diesel generators, and since late March it has relied on a single off-site power line. The war in Ukraine keeps producing new flashpoints, including the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ukraine-strikes-crimean-bridge-second-day-record-drones-20261118">second day of strikes on the Crimean bridge</a>, while world leaders prepare to discuss the conflict at the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/trump-xi-putin-first-trilateral-shenzhen-apec-20261117">first Trump Xi Putin trilateral meeting in Shenzhen</a>.</p><h2>Why it matters</h2><p>A meltdown at one plant would be a disaster for Ukraine alone. A blackout hitting nine reactors at once, as Strand warned, would be a severe situation with the potential for radioactive releases across Europe. The warning also raises the stakes for the winter ahead: Russia campaign against the grid is aimed at freezing Ukraine into concessions, but the regulators say it is creating a nuclear safety risk that no side can control once cooling fails.</p><p>The call also puts pressure on European governments to prepare. Strand said that if Russia does not stop, European countries must work together on emergency preparedness to tackle the possible consequences of a nuclear accident.</p><h2>What is not confirmed</h2><p>No nuclear accident has happened, and the warning describes a rising risk, not an event under way. The regulators did not publish a timetable for when a blackout might occur, and Russia and Ukraine accuse each other of striking nuclear plants and severing power lines, so responsibility for individual outages remains disputed. The casualty and damage figures from the wider grid campaign have not been independently verified.</p><h2>Sources</h2><p>Coverage: <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/risk-nuclear-accident-ukraine-increasing-this-winter-atomic-safety-regulators-2026-11-16/">Reuters</a>, 16 November 2026; <a href="https://nypost.com/2026/11/16/world-news/risk-of-nuclear-accident-in-ukraine-increasing-over-russian-airstrikes-officials-warn/">New York Post</a>, 16 November 2026.</p>$$,
$$<p>३८ देशका आणविक सुरक्षा नियामकहरूले सोमबार १६ नोभेम्बरमा चेतावनी दिएअनुसार यस जाडोमा युक्रेनमा आणविक दुर्घटनाको जोखिम बढिरहेको छ। उनीहरूले युक्रेनी रिएक्टरहरू चिसो राख्ने विद्युत् पूर्वाधारमा आक्रमण रोक्न रुसलाई आग्रह गरे।</p><p>यो चेतावनी HERCA, WENRA र NORA गरी तीन संस्थाको संयुक्त वक्तव्यमा आएको हो, जुन प्रकाशनअघि रोयटर्सले देखेको थियो। यी संस्थाहरूले सबै युरोपेली संघका देशहरू र अमेरिका, बेलायत, क्यानडा र जापानलगायत देशहरूको प्रतिनिधित्व गर्छन्।</p><h2>युक्रेनमा आणविक दुर्घटनाको जोखिम एक नजरमा</h2><table><tr><th>विवरण</th><th>जानकारी</th></tr><tr><td>चेतावनी</td><td>यस जाडोमा युक्रेनमा आणविक दुर्घटनाको जोखिम बढ्दै, १६ नोभेम्बर २०२६</td></tr><tr><td>जारी गर्ने</td><td>HERCA, WENRA र NORA मार्फत ३८ देशका नियामकहरू</td></tr><tr><td>मुख्य चिन्ता</td><td>अस्थिर ग्रिड विद्युतले आणविक इन्धन चिसो राख्न र मेल्टडाउन रोक्न गाह्रो बनाउँछ</td></tr><tr><td>आणविक केन्द्र</td><td>चार केन्द्र, तीन सञ्चालनमा, जाडोको विद्युतको करिब ८० प्रतिशत</td></tr><tr><td>सबैभन्दा खराब अवस्था</td><td>नौवटै उत्पादनशील रिएक्टरमा एकैपटक राष्ट्रव्यापी ब्ल्याकआउट</td></tr><tr><td>जापोरिजिया</td><td>युरोपको सबैभन्दा ठूलो केन्द्र, रुसी नियन्त्रणमा, २७ पटक बाह्य विद्युत गुमायो</td></tr></table><h2>के भयो</h2><p>मस्कोले युक्रेनी ऊर्जा स्थापनाहरू, जस्तै केन्द्र, विद्युत् लाइन र सबस्टेसनहरूमा ड्रोन र मिसाइल आक्रमण बढाएको छ। यो प्रणाली झन्डै पाँच वर्षको द्वन्द्वपछि पहिल्यै कमजोर थियो। नियामकहरूले आणविक सुविधाहरू र तिनको सुरक्षित सञ्चालनका लागि आवश्यक ऊर्जा पूर्वाधारमा आक्रमण रोक्न रुसलाई तत्काल आवश्यकता रहेको बताए।</p><p>मुख्य चिन्ता चिसो पार्ने प्रणाली हो। ग्रिडबाट स्थिर विद्युत् आपूर्ति नभए युक्रेनी आणविक केन्द्रहरूले आणविक सामग्री चिसो राख्न र मेल्टडाउन रोक्न झन् गाह्रो हुन्छ। त्यसो भए १९८६ को चेरनोबिल दुर्घटनाजस्तै वायुमण्डलमा रेडियोधर्मी पदार्थ फैलिन सक्छ, जुन विश्वकै सबैभन्दा खराब नागरिक आणविक दुर्घटना मानिन्छ।</p><p>HERCA र NORA का प्रमुख पेर स्ट्रान्डले रोयटर्सलाई भने कि पछिल्ला हप्तामा रुसी आक्रमणहरू ठूलो पेलोड भएका र बढी सटीक ड्रोन प्रयोगले घातक भएका छन्। उनले सबैभन्दा उच्च जोखिम यसै जाडोमा हुने र सबैभन्दा खराब अवस्था नौवटै रिएक्टरमा एकैपटक ब्ल्याकआउट हुने बताए। अहिले नै कदम चाले रुसलाई आक्रमण रोक्न दबाब दिए यो अवस्था रोक्न सकिने उनले जोड दिए।</p><p>शनिबार १४ नोभेम्बरमा अन्तर्राष्ट्रिय आणविक ऊर्जा एजेन्सीले सैन्य गतिविधिले अघिल्लो हप्ता युक्रेनको विद्युत् अस्थिर बनाएको र केही सञ्चालन इकाइहरूले उत्पादन घटाएको बताएको थियो। युक्रेनका चार आणविक केन्द्रमध्ये तीन सञ्चालनमा छन् र जाडोको विद्युतको करिब ८० प्रतिशत दिन्छन्। चौथो, युरोपको सबैभन्दा ठूलो जापोरिजिया, मोर्चा नजिकै रुसी नियन्त्रणमा छ। यो अहिले विद्युत् उत्पादन गरिरहेको छैन तर आणविक सामग्री चिसो राख्न बाह्य विद्युतमा निर्भर छ। युद्ध सुरु भएयता यसले २७ पटक बाह्य विद्युत गुमाएको छ।</p><h2>किन महत्त्वपूर्ण छ</h2><p>एउटा केन्द्रमा मेल्टडाउन भए युक्रेनका लागि मात्रै विपत्ति हुनेछ। स्ट्रान्डले चेतावनी दिएअनुसार नौवटै रिएक्टरमा एकैपटक ब्ल्याकआउट भए युरोपभरि रेडियोधर्मी पदार्थ फैलिने सम्भावनासहितको गम्भीर अवस्था हुनेछ। यो चेतावनीले आगामी जाडोको जोखिम बढाउँछ: ग्रिडविरुद्धको रुसको अभियान युक्रेनलाई छुट दिन बाध्य पार्ने उद्देश्यले हो, तर नियामकहरू भन्छन् यसले कुनै पक्षले नियन्त्रण गर्न नसक्ने आणविक सुरक्षा जोखिम सिर्जना गरिरहेको छ।</p><p>यो आह्वानले युरोपेली सरकारहरूमाथि तयारीको दबाब पनि बढाउँछ। रुसले नरोकिए आणविक दुर्घटनाको सम्भावित परिणाम सामना गर्न युरोपेली देशहरूले आपतकालीन तयारीमा सँगै काम गर्नुपर्ने स्ट्रान्डले बताए।</p><h2>के पुष्टि भएको छैन</h2><p>कुनै आणविक दुर्घटना भएको छैन; चेतावनीले बढ्दो जोखिम वर्णन गर्छ, भइरहेको घटना होइन। नियामकहरूले ब्ल्याकआउट कहिले हुन सक्छ भन्ने समयसीमा प्रकाशित गरेका छैनन्, र रुस र युक्रेनले एकअर्कालाई आणविक केन्द्रमा आक्रमण र विद्युत् लाइन काटेको आरोप लगाउँछन्, त्यसैले व्यक्तिगत अवरोधको जिम्मेवारी विवादित छ।</p><h2>स्रोतहरू</h2><p>रिपोर्ट: <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/risk-nuclear-accident-ukraine-increasing-this-winter-atomic-safety-regulators-2026-11-16/">Reuters</a>, १६ नोभेम्बर २०२६; <a href="https://nypost.com/2026/11/16/world-news/risk-of-nuclear-accident-in-ukraine-increasing-over-russian-airstrikes-officials-warn/">New York Post</a>, १६ नोभेम्बर २०२६।</p>$$,
